     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose isn't safe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times a day comes to 6,400 mg a day.\n- The usual highest single dose for adults is 800 mg, so 1,600 mg is twice that.\n- The usual highest daily total for adults on prescription is 3,200 mg, so this order is twice that too.\n\nAt this level the risk of stomach bleeding, kidney injury, and heart problems goes up significantly, even in someone with normal kidney and liver function. It looks like it could be a typo, a mix-up between the per-dose and per-day amount, or a mistake made when the order was passed along.\n\n**2. A prescriber needs to place it.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber who is responsible for this patient's care. That keeps the order legally valid and makes sure someone is accountable for the decision.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Get in touch with the patient's attending physician or the medicine team. They can confirm what was intended and place the order themselves.\n- If they want ibuprofen, a typical adult order is 400\u2013800 mg by mouth every 6\u20138 hours as needed, up to 3,200 mg a day. They'll pick the right dose for this patient.\n\nI can enter the order once a prescriber confirms the dose and is the one placing it."
      }
     ],
